Helena Yearwood &lt;hyearwood@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Jonathan Simon &lt;Jonathan.Simon@wtwco.com&gt;Cc:&nbsp;Emissions Case Leads &lt;EmissionsCaseLeads@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Alicia Alinia &lt;aalinia@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Subject:&nbsp;RE: Pan NOx Notification&nbsp;CAUTION: This email originated from outside of the organization. Do not click links or open attachments unless you recognize the sender and know the content is safe.&nbsp;Thanks Michael. This is with insurers. We have asked them to get back to us asap given the PTR on Monday.&nbsp;We will be in touch with their response.&nbsp;Kind regards,&nbsp;James Burke&nbsp;Associate Director (Solicitor)Litigation and Contingent Risk FINEX Global&nbsp;WTWOn behalf of Willis Limited, 51 Lime Street, London, EC3M 7DQ (registered number 181116)On behalf of Willis Towers Watson SA/NV, Quai des Vennes, 4020, Liège, Belgium (registered as a branch in the UK at 51 Lime Street, London, EC3M 7DQ UK Branch Number BR021056) in relation to all EEA-regulated business&nbsp;D: +44 (0)20 31248037M: +44 (0)7831 162591&nbsp;james.burke2@wtwco.comwww.wtwco.com&nbsp;Financial, Professional, and Executive Risks (FINEX)Your Trusted Partner in Managing Complex Risk: Expertly navigating Litigation Risk, Cyber, Directors &amp; Officers, Financial Institutions, Mergers &amp; Acquisitions, Professional Indemnity, and Operational Risks within WTW.Part of our wider risk solutions capabilities at WTW&nbsp;At WTW we work flexibly - whilst you may receive emails from me out of working hours, I do not expect a response or action outside your own working hours.&nbsp;&nbsp;From:&nbsp;Michael Burke &lt;mburke@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Sent:&nbsp;03 October 2025 12:20To:&nbsp;James Burke &lt;james.burke2@wtwco.com&gt;; Helena Yearwood &lt;hyearwood@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Jonathan Simon &lt;jonathan.simon@wtwco.com&gt;Cc:&nbsp;Emissions Case Leads &lt;EmissionsCaseLeads@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Alicia Alinia &lt;aalinia@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Subject:&nbsp;Fw: Pan NOx Notification&nbsp;Hi James,&nbsp;Good to speak to you yesterday. As discussed, these applications were launched, and then heard, on an incredibly compressed timescale, at a time when there were i) a number of competing other critical path deadlines and ii) significant managerial changes and upheaval within PG. We apologise that the insurers were not put on notice sooner. On a positive note, and as per the attached, Counsel have advised that they do not feel that prospects of success have been materially altered as a result of these applications.&nbsp;&nbsp;To confirm, we need to try and reach agreement with the Defendants on these costs today&nbsp;to avoid them being aired at the PTR on Monday.&nbsp;In terms of the timetable for when these applications were made to when judgement was handed down:&nbsp;Defendants raised concerns over the content of the reports of Dr Heitz at the PTR on 29 July;Defendants crystallised those concerns in correspondences on 8 August;Claimants replied with detailed responses on 20 August;Applications were made by the Defendants (without response to our 20 August letter) on 27 August;Witness evidence for the applications was filed 2 September;Skeletons were filed 8 SeptemberHearing 10 September and oral judgement handed down 11 September.&nbsp;&nbsp;The timeframe for these applications also overlapped with finalisation of the supplemental expert reports and the substantive PDD Trial skeleton. We have since been provided with cost schedules by the Defendants for us to consider them with the schedules still being updated even to date. The costs sought by the Defendants are as set out in the below table, with PG's proportionate share of those costs in red:&nbsp;ReportMercedes 29.84%Renault 44.42%Nissan 41.08%Ford 49.29%TotalHeitz£392,755.71 £117,198.30£124,867.13 £55,465.98£421,034.11&nbsp;£172,960.81£212,398.41 £104,691.18£1,151,055.36 £450,316.27Tate£70,543.30 £21,050.12£15,478.82 £6,875.69&nbsp;£24,947.94 £12,296.84£110,970.06&nbsp;£40,222.65Smithers&nbsp;£23,719.02 £10,535.99&nbsp;£31,421.72 £15,487.77£55,140.74 £26,023.76Glow Plugs&nbsp;£257,703.57 £114,471.92£315,388.56 £129,561.62&nbsp;£573,092.13 £244,033.54TOTAL£463,299.01&nbsp;£138,248.42£421,768.54 £187,349.58&nbsp;£736,422.67 £302,522.43£268,768.07 £132,475.79£1,890,258.29&nbsp;£&nbsp;On the issue of glow plugs, these were notified to the insurers on 18 August (per the attached). While not strictly for settlement at the PTR, the Renault and Nissan Defendants have made it clear that they need these costs to be considered as part of any deal they will do on the costs of the applications. We will attempt to resist this but it is prudent to seek approval now to allow us to negotiate.&nbsp;&nbsp;The Defendants have made it clear that should the parties be unable to reach an agreement, they will be seeking the Court make an order of indemnity costs against the Claimants at the PTR on Monday 6 October. The approach taken by so far has been to sound out the Defendants as to whether we can reach an agreement that avoids them making such an application. LD have sent a WP offer to Mercedes, &quot;offer[ing] either (i) 66% in relation to your clients’ statement of costs in respect of the Dr Heitz application, payable within 28 days, or (ii) an interim payment of your indemnity costs of 50% now, payable within 28 days, with the remainder to be assessed.&quot;&nbsp;We do not think this is something that will be acceptable across all of the Defendants, not least because there are nuances to how &quot;successful&quot; each Defendant was in their application.&nbsp;&nbsp;We would like to request authority to go to 80% of the due costs of the Heitz reports, 80% of the costs of the Smithers costs, 80% of the Glow Plugs costs, and for us to push the Tate costs to be Costs in the Case. This would equate to:&nbsp;ReportMercedesRenaultNissanFordTotalHeitz£93,758.64£44,372.78£138,368.65£83,752.94£360,253.01Tate&nbsp;Smithers&nbsp;£8,428.79&nbsp;£12,390.22£20,819.01Glow Plugs&nbsp;£91,577.54£103,649.30&nbsp;TOTAL£93,758.64£144,379.11£242,017.95£96,143.16£576,298.86&nbsp;If we can be of any further assistance, please let us know.&nbsp;&nbsp;Thanks,&nbsp;Pogust Goodhead&nbsp;From:&nbsp;James Burke &lt;james.burke2@wtwco.com&gt;Sent:&nbsp;Wednesday, October 01, 2025 6:28 PMTo:&nbsp;Helena Yearwood &lt;hyearwood@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Michael Burke &lt;mburke@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Jonathan Simon &lt;Jonathan.Simon@wtwco.com&gt;Cc:&nbsp;Emissions Case Leads &lt;EmissionsCaseLeads@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Subject:&nbsp;RE: Pan NOx Notification&nbsp;CAUTION: This email originated from outside of the organization. Do not click links or open attachments unless you recognize the sender and know the content is safe.&nbsp;Hi Helena,&nbsp;Apologies for missing your calls, I was in a meeting this afternoon.&nbsp;For the moment insurers have reserved their rights in respect of any claim on the policy. They have also asked whether PG previously informed insurers about these applications and/or sought consent to contest them?&nbsp;Would you be able to give more information about the timing and making of the applications, the decision to contest them and their notice to insurers? I can see that there have been references to the expert reports in the periodic NOx updates but no express mention of the Defendants’ applications.&nbsp;The i.) Mercedes, and ii.) NOx wrapper policies have slightly different exclusion clauses in relation to adverse costs attributable to interim applications.&nbsp;The Mercedes policy excludes any claim under the policy attributable to “any interim application made or resisted without Insurer’s Approval” (clause 2.1.4).&nbsp;The NOx wrapper policies (Ford, RN and CP) excludes, unless Insurers Approval has been given, any claim under the policy attributable to:“The Insured’s intention to apply for summary judgment under CPR Part 24 or to make any other application which will be listed for or take half a day or more” (clause 4.1.4)&nbsp;The insurers have not yet sought to rely on these clauses but I would imagine these clauses are the context in which they seek further information. Given clause 4.1.4 above, it may also be that a notification was not required under the policy in any event.&nbsp;Regarding your instructions to Counsel – can you let us know the offer to settle the matters that you would like us to put to insurers for their agreement?&nbsp;Kind regards,&nbsp;James Burke&nbsp;Associate Director (Solicitor)Litigation and Contingent Risk FINEX Global&nbsp;WTWOn behalf of Willis Limited, 51 Lime Street, London, EC3M 7DQ (registered number 181116)On behalf of Willis Towers Watson SA/NV, Quai des Vennes, 4020, Liège, Belgium (registered as a branch in the UK at 51 Lime Street, London, EC3M 7DQ UK Branch Number BR021056) in relation to all EEA-regulated business&nbsp;D: +44 (0)20 31248037M: +44 (0)7831 162591&nbsp;james.burke2@wtwco.comwww.wtwco.com&nbsp;Financial, Professional, and Executive Risks (FINEX)Your Trusted Partner in Managing Complex Risk: Expertly navigating Litigation Risk, Cyber, Directors &amp; Officers, Financial Institutions, Mergers &amp; Acquisitions, Professional Indemnity, and Operational Risks within WTW.Part of our wider risk solutions capabilities at WTW&nbsp;At WTW we work flexibly - whilst you may receive emails from me out of working hours, I do not expect a response or action outside your own working hours.&nbsp;&nbsp;From:&nbsp;Helena Yearwood &lt;hyearwood@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Sent:&nbsp;01 October 2025 15:07To:&nbsp;James Burke &lt;james.burke2@wtwco.com&gt;; Michael Burke &lt;mburke@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Jonathan Simon &lt;Jonathan.Simon@wtwco.com&gt;Cc:&nbsp;Emissions Case Leads &lt;EmissionsCaseLeads@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Subject:&nbsp;RE: Pan NOx Notification&nbsp;Dear James,&nbsp;Further to Michael’s email yesterday, we are keen to finalise our position on the offers that can be made so that we can proceed with instructing Counsel.&nbsp;Would you be available for a brief call this afternoon to discuss next steps?&nbsp;Kind regardsHelena&nbsp;​​​​Helena&nbsp;YearwoodRisk&nbsp;&amp;&nbsp;Compliance&nbsp;DirectorThis email and any attachments are confidential and may also be privileged. If you receive this message in error, please contact the sender immediately, destroy the email and any attachments and do not use, copy, store or disclose this email and any attachments for any purpose. Pogust Goodhead does not accept service of documents by electronic means without express prior agreement.&nbsp;Pogust Goodhead is the trading name of&nbsp;PGMBM Law Ltd, a&nbsp;limited liability company registered in England and Wales (no. 6284764) with its&nbsp;registered office at&nbsp;53-64 Chancery House, Chancery Lane, London WC2A 1QS&nbsp;and authorised and regulated by the Solicitors' Regulation Authority. We use the term ‘partner’ to refer to an employee or consultant of Pogust Goodhead with equivalent standing or qualifications. No&nbsp;reference to a partner should be taken to imply partnership for the purposes of the Partnership Act 1890&nbsp;or Limited Liability Partnerships Act 2000. A list of partners is available on our website.&nbsp;For details about what personal information we collect and why, please see our Privacy Policy.From:&nbsp;James Burke &lt;james.burke2@wtwco.com&gt;Sent:&nbsp;30 September 2025 17:18To:&nbsp;Michael Burke &lt;mburke@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Jonathan Simon &lt;Jonathan.Simon@wtwco.com&gt;Cc:&nbsp;Helena Yearwood &lt;hyearwood@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Emissions Case Leads &lt;EmissionsCaseLeads@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Subject:&nbsp;RE: Pan NOx Notification&nbsp;CAUTION: This email originated from outside of the organization. Do not click links or open attachments unless you recognize the sender and know the content is safe.&nbsp;Many thanks for your email Michael. We will notify insurers in relation to the potential adverse cost risk set out below.&nbsp;Should you have any further queries or require anything further at this stage, please let us know.&nbsp;Kind regards,&nbsp;James Burke&nbsp;Associate Director (Solicitor)Litigation and Contingent Risk FINEX Global&nbsp;WTWOn behalf of Willis Limited, 51 Lime Street, London, EC3M 7DQ (registered number 181116)On behalf of Willis Towers Watson SA/NV, Quai des Vennes, 4020, Liège, Belgium (registered as a branch in the UK at 51 Lime Street, London, EC3M 7DQ UK Branch Number BR021056) in relation to all EEA-regulated business&nbsp;D: +44 (0)20 31248037M: +44 (0)7831 162591&nbsp;james.burke2@wtwco.comwww.wtwco.com&nbsp;Financial, Professional, and Executive Risks (FINEX)Your Trusted Partner in Managing Complex Risk: Expertly navigating Litigation Risk, Cyber, Directors &amp; Officers, Financial Institutions, Mergers &amp; Acquisitions, Professional Indemnity, and Operational Risks within WTW.Part of our wider risk solutions capabilities at WTW&nbsp;At WTW we work flexibly - whilst you may receive emails from me out of working hours, I do not expect a response or action outside your own working hours.&nbsp;&nbsp;From:&nbsp;Michael Burke &lt;mburke@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Sent:&nbsp;30 September 2025 14:31To:&nbsp;James Burke &lt;james.burke2@wtwco.com&gt;; Jonathan Simon &lt;jonathan.simon@wtwco.com&gt;Cc:&nbsp;Helena Yearwood &lt;hyearwood@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Emissions Case Leads &lt;EmissionsCaseLeads@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Subject:&nbsp;Pan NOx Notification&nbsp;Dear James, Jonathan,&nbsp;I hope you are both well.&nbsp;&nbsp;I am writing on behalf of the Mercedes, Ford, Renault/Nissan and Peugeot/Citroën NOx Litigations to update you on a series of applications that have been made by the Defendants, and an Order by the Court.&nbsp;&nbsp;There were applications made in respect of expert reports we have obtained in these Litigations, 1 being in relation to the automotive software expert reports prepared and filed by Dr Markus Heitz (the Claimant's automotive software expert); 2 being in respect of aspects of the report prepared by the Dr Tate (the Mercedes Claimant's Normal Driving Conditions expert); and 3 in respect of a generic section of the report of Juston Smithers (the Mercedes Claimant's automotive engineering expert). Substantive, and supplementary, reports were prepared and filed by each of these experts over July and August 2025.&nbsp;&nbsp;The applications made by the Defendants were largely on similar grounds: they argued that Dr Heitz stepped outside the remit of his instruction and provided expert evidence that should be left to the mechanical engineer i.e. he provided duplicative evidence; that the Claimants did not have permission to rely on the evidence of Dr Tate in any litigation other than Mercedes; and that the Claimants did not have permission to rely on the evidence of Mr Smithers in any litigation other than Mercedes. These applications were heard on a one day hearing on 10 September 2025 and while we did seek to reach agreement where we could, largely they were opposed.&nbsp;In her judgment, Mrs Justice Cockerill was scathing in its criticism of the reports, seemingly displaying a real unhappiness over the fact this hearing was even required, but made some notable points about what the reports could continue to have in them (which were helpful for the Claimants).&nbsp;&nbsp;As a result of these &quot;lost&quot; applications, the Defendants made the point that they would be seeking their costs of the applications on an indemnity basis. While the Court did not indicate it would award costs on an indemnity basis, our suspicion is that if we do not reach an agreement with the Defendants for the total amount of costs to be paid, and the Defendants make an application for a costs order on a indemnity basis, they will be successful.&nbsp;The costs sought by the Defendants are"}

