t into the equivalent position of a 2nd/3rd/4th layer insurer (as applicable).Toremis are aware of the pre-existing cross-collateralisation provisions (Nathan Hull was head of ATE at VALE when these documents were put in place). They are comfortable with the fact that they would sit below these (as to do otherwise would need to unwind the provisions that are in place).@Anna Varga @Matt Hunt,@Matthew Newbould Re the merits call later – Toremis have no particular queries the case leads would need to be aware of. It will essentially be fleshing out the bullet-point queries sent to Counsel (below) / giving a “state of the nation” walk-through of the merits of the matter, similar to the call conducted with the existing insurers ahead of the PDD trial.&nbsp;Confirming, at a high level, that, with the benefit of having now reviewed the expert and factual evidence (and the detailed legal position taken by the defendants) the merits remain at least as good as the original assessments (including confirming what the relevant percentage assessment is for each of the defendants).Confirming the position on the merits in relation to: (1) whether each of the defendants' vehicles contained unlawful defeat devices; (2) whether there are any viable article 5 defences available to the defendants; and (3) to the extent separate to (1) and (2), the position on the existence of a breach of statutory duty and/or a breach of contract.&nbsp;We also spoke to them about this morning’s piece in the Times. They were not too concerned about it as they did not view it as affecting the underlying merits of the case. We do not think it will come up on today’s call.&nbsp;I hope that assists. I will come back to you with your queries re Mosaic separately as I wanted to get these points to you and the team soonest ahead of the Toremis call.&nbsp;Kind regards,&nbsp;James Burke&nbsp;Associate Director (Solicitor)Litigation and Contingent Risk FINEX Global &nbsp;WTWOn behalf of Willis Limited, 51 Lime Str