Dear all,
&nbsp;
Many thanks to all for the chronology and table. I have discussed with OC and we do not think there is a perfect, good or clean solution here. We are seeking to identify the least worst option. In the circumstances, we think the least
 worst option is to provide copies of the written instructions to Mercedes and PCD to address their current application. However, we doubt this will shut down the dispute and we can expect it to resurface at the second&nbsp;PTR.
&nbsp;
In brief:
&nbsp;
1.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;
We think Cockerill J is likely to direct production of the written instructions, for reasons already discussed.
2.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;
Production of the written instructions will not resolve the issue finally because in Merc the written instructions were quite narrow, but Heitz's account of his instructions is broader, reflecting the wider task he in fact carried out. In contrast, in
 PCD Heitz’s instructions were much broader and sent to him just before his report was served.&nbsp;
3.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;
It will therefore be suggested that there must have been further oral instructions at least in Merc. We will respond by saying that Dr Heitz had a broad understanding of his proper task, and we did not suggest to him otherwise but there were no such
 instructions.&nbsp;
&nbsp;
We think we will need to deal with the inevitable supplemental questions as best we can, but this approach will avoid any suggestion of concealment of written instructions. We cannot eliminate the problem
 entirely, and there will be further applications, but this seems to be the best of a number of unwelcome options.
&nbsp;
Could we have instructions?
