en delete it from your system. You should not copy it or use it for any purpose, nor disclose its contents to any other person. Please also note that emails sent or received may be monitored to ensure compliance with the law and with our policies.&nbsp;Further regulatory information can be found in the Legal Notices which appear on our website at&nbsp;https://www.pallasllp.com/legal.​​​​From: Tom Crow &lt;tcrow@pogustgoodhead.com&gt; Sent: 10 October 2025 08:14To: Neil Pigott &lt;Neil.Pigott@pallasllp.com&gt;; Carolina Koschdoski de Souza &lt;csouza@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Cc: Helena Yearwood &lt;hyearwood@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Anna Varga &lt;avarga@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Jessica Fechner &lt;jfechner@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Subject: RE: Defendants’ Submissions and Draft Order – Costs of CMH2&nbsp;Morning All &nbsp;I have slightly tweaked the letter as attached (amend for repetition and raise one comment). On Theo’s comments (focussing on those that require further thought and comment our side)&nbsp;I haven't been party to the detail of Gramercy's arrangements with PG save that I have seen what is in Alinia 1, so can't help on whether this detail is correct/comprehensive. What is important to say is that given the ATE shortfall there is a real risk that the funding agreements between PG and Gramercy will eventually be disclosed to the court. It is critical that this account presents a straightforward and accurate picture of Gramercy's funding.We are checking internally to make sure that we are comfortable with the statement that “the September ($30m) Tranche can contractually be used only for the NOx Group Litigations.” I believe this is correct on the basis that the facility must be spent in line with the funder approved budget and that budget only provides for spend against the Nox litigation and proportionate share of firm operating costs, but I am not sure how clear this will be to demonstrate through the actual contracts.&nbsp;You need to be careful that this is absolutely aligned with what is said about the Gramercy additional funding in Alicia Alinia’s recent First Witness Statement. Particularly she says the 30m additional is reserved to only be spent on these proceedings while the 65m is also for wider overheads. We need to be consistent here.@Neil Pigott ?&nbsp;Are there any other funders of the litigation, including historic, who might be said to contribute to the Claimant’s costs? I distantly recall I have seen mention of another funder - but is that historic?We said the below in Tom’s March witness statement, this would have been in relation to NorthWall. &nbsp;The funds provided by Gramercy were used, in part, to repay previous debt finance provided to Pogust Goodhead, which has been superseded by the funding.&nbsp;We have not disclosed the $140m G2 facility which is exhausted and did not provide funding for the Nox litigation (save for overheads in the same way as G3)&nbsp;Other potentially relevant funders which we discussed yesterday include Locke, Fulcrum, DRS, and Pogust Goodhead LLC, who provided funding for Nox client aggregation costs (marketing) &nbsp;&nbsp;Tom&nbsp;&nbsp;​​​​Tom&nbsp;CrowGroup&nbsp;Financial&nbsp;Controller+44&nbsp;(0)&nbsp;7890&nbsp;043&nbsp;708This email and any attachments are confidential and may also be privileged. If you receive this message in error, please contact the sender immediately, destroy the email and any attachments and do not use, copy, store or disclose this email and any attachments for any purpose. Pogust Goodhead does not accept service of documents by electronic means without express prior agreement.&nbsp;Pogust Goodhead is the trading name of&nbsp;PGMBM Law Ltd, a&nbsp;limited liability company registered in England and Wales (no. 6284764) with its&nbsp;registered office at&nbsp;53-64 Chancery House, Chancery Lane, London WC2A 1QS&nbsp;and authorised and regulated by the Solicitors' Regulation Authority. We use the term ‘partner’ to refer to an employee or consultant of Pogust Goodhead with equivalent standing or qualifications. No&nbsp;reference to a partner should be taken to imply partnership for the purposes of the Partnership Act 1890&nbsp;or Limited Liability Partnerships Act 2000. A list of partners is available on our&nbsp;website.&nbsp;For details about what personal information we collect and why, please see our&nbsp;Privacy Policy.From: Tom Crow &lt;tcrow@pogustgoodhead.com&gt; Sent: 10 October 2025 07:02To: Neil Pigott &lt;neil.pigott@pallasllp.com&gt;Cc: Helena Yearwood &lt;hyearwood@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Anna Varga &lt;avarga@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Jessica Fechner &lt;jfechner@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Subject: FW: Defendants’ Submissions and Draft Order – Costs of CMH2&nbsp;Morning Neil&nbsp;See response from Theo Barclay (counsel that will be dealing with the November funding disclosure applications)&nbsp;I have not had chance to read this yet &nbsp;Tom&nbsp;​​​​Tom&nbsp;CrowGroup&nbsp;Financial&nbsp;Controller+44&nbsp;(0)&nbsp;7890&nbsp;043&nbsp;708From: Theo Barclay &lt;T.Barclay@4newsquare.com&gt; Sent: 09 October 2025 22:09To: Tom Crow &lt;tcrow@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; S.Teasdale@4newsquare.comCc: B.Williams@4newsquare.com; Anna Varga &lt;avarga@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Jessica Fechner &lt;jfechner@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Helena Yearwood &lt;hyearwood@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Subject: Re: Defendants’ Submissions and Draft Order – Costs of CMH2&nbsp;CAUTION: This email originated from outside of the organization. Do not click links or open attachments unless you recognize the sender and know the content is safe.&nbsp;Dear Tom,&nbsp;Please see attached a draft of the letter with my suggested amendments and comments.&nbsp;The most important point here is to make sure that what is said about PG’s funding agreements is accurate and not misleading by omission, because anything that isn’t quite right (or a relevant funding arrangement not included) will doubtless be seized on down the line.&nbsp;On the ATE assumptions, my views are as follows (and I know these are shared by Simon):&nbsp;We think that future costs for ADR and Experts should be included in the relevant expenditure calculations. Despite not having been assessed by the court yet, those costs would be incurred within the relevant period and therefore will be taken into account when assessing of sufficiency of cover. It would be reasonable at this stage to take the Claimants’ offers against those phases as the starting point for what we say is required, but we must recognise that leaving those phases out altogether isn’t going to be defensible before the court. So if para 9a and 9b are to go into the letter (and I can see they may be needed for now if the position on sufficiency of cover is to be maintained) we need to accept internally that there will need to be movement on that point (and additional cover) if the position on ATE sufficiency is to be maintained before the court.&nbsp;Similarly, we don’t think there is a strong case for downgrading the % of incurred costs by 10%. Again, if this is to go into the letter we ought to treat it internally as a holding position while you look to increase cover.&nbsp;We agree that it is not sensible to disclose in the letter that you are looking to increase cover. Most importantly, that essentially amounts to a concession that what you have is insufficient - and additionally, as you point out, you may not be able to secure further cover and we don’t want to box ourselves in.&nbsp;I hope that helps. I am around tomorrow morning if needed until around 1pm.&nbsp;Many thanks,&nbsp;Theo&nbsp;Theo&nbsp;Barclay​​​​BarristerDirect&nbsp;+442078222100&nbsp;Mobile&nbsp;+447411&nbsp;731074&nbsp;Chambers&nbsp;+442078222000&nbsp;4&nbsp;New&nbsp;Square&nbsp;Chambers​Lincoln's&nbsp;Inn,&nbsp;London&nbsp;WC2A&nbsp;3RJFollow&nbsp;us&nbsp;on&nbsp;YouTube&nbsp;|&nbsp;LinkedIn​Discover&nbsp;our&nbsp;Events&nbsp;Privilege and Confidentiality Notice:This email is private as between the sender and 