ivalent standing or qualifications. No&nbsp;reference to a partner should be taken to imply partnership for the purposes of the Partnership Act 1890&nbsp;or Limited Liability Partnerships Act 2000. A list of partners is available on our website.&nbsp;For details about what personal information we collect and why, please see our Privacy Policy.From:&nbsp;James Burke &lt;james.burke2@wtwco.com&gt;Sent:&nbsp;30 September 2025 17:18To:&nbsp;Michael Burke &lt;mburke@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Jonathan Simon &lt;Jonathan.Simon@wtwco.com&gt;Cc:&nbsp;Helena Yearwood &lt;hyearwood@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Emissions Case Leads &lt;EmissionsCaseLeads@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Subject:&nbsp;RE: Pan NOx Notification&nbsp;CAUTION: This email originated from outside of the organization. Do not click links or open attachments unless you recognize the sender and know the content is safe.&nbsp;Many thanks for your email Michael. We will notify insurers in relation to the potential adverse cost risk set out below.&nbsp;Should you have any further queries or require anything further at this stage, please let us know.&nbsp;Kind regards,&nbsp;James Burke&nbsp;Associate Director (Solicitor)Litigation and Contingent Risk FINEX Global&nbsp;WTWOn behalf of Willis Limited, 51 Lime Street, London, EC3M 7DQ (registered number 181116)On behalf of Willis Towers Watson SA/NV, Quai des Vennes, 4020, Liège, Belgium (registered as a branch in the UK at 51 Lime Street, London, EC3M 7DQ UK Branch Number BR021056) in relation to all EEA-regulated business&nbsp;D: +44 (0)20 31248037M: +44 (0)7831 162591&nbsp;james.burke2@wtwco.comwww.wtwco.com&nbsp;Financial, Professional, and Executive Risks (FINEX)Your Trusted Partner in Managing Complex Risk: Expertly navigating Litigation Risk, Cyber, Directors &amp; Officers, Financial Institutions, Mergers &amp; Acquisitions, Professional Indemnity, and Operational Risks within WTW.Part of our wider risk solutions capabilities at WTW&nbsp;At WTW we work flexibly - whilst you may receive emails from me out of working hours, I do not expect a response or action outside your own working hours.&nbsp;&nbsp;From:&nbsp;Michael Burke &lt;mburke@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Sent:&nbsp;30 September 2025 14:31To:&nbsp;James Burke &lt;james.burke2@wtwco.com&gt;; Jonathan Simon &lt;jonathan.simon@wtwco.com&gt;Cc:&nbsp;Helena Yearwood &lt;hyearwood@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Emissions Case Leads &lt;EmissionsCaseLeads@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Subject:&nbsp;Pan NOx Notification&nbsp;Dear James, Jonathan,&nbsp;I hope you are both well.&nbsp;&nbsp;I am writing on behalf of the Mercedes, Ford, Renault/Nissan and Peugeot/Citroën NOx Litigations to update you on a series of applications that have been made by the Defendants, and an Order by the Court.&nbsp;&nbsp;There were applications made in respect of expert reports we have obtained in these Litigations, 1 being in relation to the automotive software expert reports prepared and filed by Dr Markus Heitz (the Claimant's automotive software expert); 2 being in respect of aspects of the report prepared by the Dr Tate (the Mercedes Claimant's Normal Driving Conditions expert); and 3 in respect of a generic section of the report of Juston Smithers (the Mercedes Claimant's automotive engineering expert). Substantive, and supplementary, reports were prepared and filed by each of these experts over July and August 2025.&nbsp;&nbsp;The applications made by the Defendants were largely on similar grounds: they argued that Dr Heitz stepped outside the remit of his instruction and provided expert evidence that should be left to the mechanical engineer i.e. he provided duplicative evidence; that the Claimants did not have permission to rely on the evidence of Dr Tate in any litigation other than Mercedes; and that the Claimants did not have permission to rely on the evidence of Mr Smithers in any litigation other than Mercedes. These applications were heard on a one day hearing on 10 September 2025 and while we did seek to reach agreement where we could, largely they were opposed.&nbsp;In her judgment, Mrs Justice Cockerill was scathing in its criticism of the reports, seemingly displaying a real unhappiness over the fact this hearing was even required, but made some notable points about what the reports could continue to have in them (which were helpful for the Claimants).&nbsp;&nbsp;As a result of these &quot;lost&quot; applications, the Defendants made the point that they would be seeking their costs of the applications on an indemnity basis. While the Court did not indicate it would award costs on an indemnity basis, our suspicion is that if we do not reach an agreement with the Defendants for the total amount of costs to be paid, and the Defendants make an application for a costs order on a indemnity basis, they will be successful.&nbsp;The costs sought by the Defendants are as follows:&nbsp;&nbsp;ReportMercedesRenaultNissanFordPCDTotalHeitz£392,755.71£124,867.13£421,034.11£212,398.41£175,000£1,326,055.36Tate£70,543.30£15,478.82&nbsp;£24,947.94£35,000£145,970.06Smithers&nbsp;£23,719.02&nbsp;£31,421.72£35,000£90,140.74TOTAL£463,299.01£164,064.97£421,034.11£268,768.07£245,000£1,562,166.16&nbsp;&nbsp;The applications (and costs pursuant to those applications) as they relate to the reports of Dr Tate and Mr Smithers, we are seeking be pushed off and be costs in the case. The Defendants were not wholly successful in those applications. We think we will be able to agree this with the Defendants.&nbsp;In respect of the applications as they relate to Dr Heitz however, we have significant concerns that if we are unable to reach an agreement with the Defendants, and they make an application to the Court, they will be awarded costs on an indemnity basis. We are today meeting on a Without Prejudice basis with the Defendants to seek to find agreement with them in respect of costs.&nbsp;&nbsp;To make it clear - PG is not and will never be responsible for 100% of these costs, only their proportionate share as per the number of claimants on the group register in each litigation.&nbsp;&nbsp;For the avoidance of doubt, as a result of the information being struck out from Dr Heitz's reports, we do not consider that there has been an adverse impact on the merits of the case.&nbsp;&nbsp;Helena will be in touch to arrange a meeting later this week to discuss this, and the wider case, in more detail. We will also provide you with a further update once we have spoken to the Defendants.&nbsp;&nbsp;Best,&nbsp;Michael BurkePogust Goodhead&nbsp;​​​​Michael&nbsp;Burke(He/Him)Partner+44&nbsp;(0)&nbsp;7960&nbsp;032&nbsp;34407960&nbsp;032&nbsp;344This email and any attachments are confidential and may also be privileged. If you receive this message in error, please contact the sender immediately, destroy the email and any attachments and do not use, copy, store or disclose this email and any attachments for any purpose. Pogust Goodhead does not accept service of documents by electronic means without express prior agreement.&nbsp;Pogust Goodhead is the trading name of&nbsp;PGMBM Law Ltd, a&nbsp;limited liability company registered in England and Wales (no. 6284764) with its&nbsp;registered office at&nbsp;53-64 Chancery House, Chancery Lane, London WC2A 1QS&nbsp;and authorised and regulated by the Solicitors' Regulation Authority. We use the term ‘partner’ to refer to an employee or consultant of Pogust Goodhead with equivalent standing or qualifications. No&nbsp;reference to a partner should be taken to imply partnership for the purposes of the Partnership Act 1890&nbsp;or Limited Liability Partnerships Act 2000. A list of partners is available on our website.&nbsp;For details about what personal information we collect and why, please see our Privacy Policy.&nbsp;______________________________________________________________________For information pertaining to WTW's email confidentiality and monitoring policy, usage restrictions, or for specific company registration and regul