Hi Anna,
&nbsp;
Following on from yesterday and the calls with LD, a few things we would suggest are raised with&nbsp;Martyn. Firstly, it is important
 to say the Case Leads and PG team are wildly committed to this trial. That should not be in question. However, the AA (the executed version of which the case leads were only sent at 9.22AM this morning), which has been referred to in Court documents and has
 been incepted without the consent of those persons it applies to (either expressly in the case of the Case Leads or, as far as the wider team is concerned, by implication), causes concern. Additionally, the case leads have also raised serious concerns about
 the staff's &nbsp;mental health that are yet to be addressed by the firm.
&nbsp;
The AA was incepted with little to no visibility or consideration for how it will practically work (as is clear given Martyn's
 explanation in ​the call on 8 October was substantially different to our understanding of what it means, a view which we consider you, also on that call, shared). We need further clarity on the AA. This is true for both for the Case Leads, and the PG staff
 who will be indirectly subject to it - by way of taking instructions from the Case Leads who are under the supervision of Leigh Day. &nbsp;As we have already told you, there is a real risk of the substantive PG team being entirely alienated by this agreement. These
 concerns were evidently ignored (including our concerns over the mental health and well-being of the staff) given it was executed without further recourse to us. &nbsp;It also remains unclear what, if any, interaction LD is to have with the wider PG team, if they
 are not subject to the AA (and therefore direction and supervision), and whether, given this, the PG team would need to revert to one of the Case Leads for every interaction, instruction, discussion had with the LD team. An obvious example of this is the trial
 bundle/logistics workstreams which have largely been left between the excellent PG associates and LD with limited input from the case leads. It is fair to say the PG team carries that workstream and LD are unprepared to take it on. Similarly with Opus and
 issues at this morning's hearing.&nbsp;
&nbsp;
It would be helpful for an explanation, and for the physical AA, to be sent out to the wider PG team&nbsp;along with some practical&nbsp;guidance
 about what it means. It would be inappropriate for the Case Leads to do this given: i) we haven’t agreed to the AA; and ii) it has been incepted against our consent.
&nbsp;
It would be helpful if the explanation covered:
&nbsp;


what LD expect from the PG team&nbsp; - do they simply expect the PG teams to &quot;do what they're told, when they're told&quot;, or is there
 still going to be a collaborative approach (the former seems more likely given how things have happened over the last few days);&nbsp;



how the &quot;do what they're told, when they're told&quot; works - or, indeed wider interaction works, in circumstances
 where the team isn't caught by the agreement. Will they need to take each instruction and run it past a Case Lead?



what happens in circumstances where the PG teams disagree with LDs position on decisions? How does this get resolved between
 the firms? We stress this is not a hypothetical. Aside from wider case strategy we have had immediate differences in opinion last week in terms of costs, with decisions being made and taken without reference to PG. This impacts all claimants, and, of course
 PG's clients. Further, in terms of wider points, as we have pointed out, LD do not have clients on some of the secondary or ALGLO cases, all of which are bound by the PDD trial judgment. It is easy to anticipate a diversion of opinion/strategy as the PDD trial
