Hi Anna,

&nbsp;
We have been giving some thought to whether the Claimant Committee’s instructions are required in order for you to step
 down from your role as Lead Solicitor. There is nothing in the engagement letters, claimant committee agreement or LGA that speaks to this – but frankly, we wouldn’t expect there to be.
In our view, this question turns on two factors:


The obligations of a solicitor (as well as agency law in general), as set out in:



SRA Principle 7 – the duty to act in the best interests of each client;
 and 
Paragraph 3.1 of the

SRA Code of Conduct for Solicitors, RELs, RFLs and RSLs, which provides:
“You only act for&nbsp;clients&nbsp;on instructions from the&nbsp;client,
 or from someone properly authorised to provide instructions on their behalf. If you have reason to suspect that the instructions do not represent your&nbsp;client's&nbsp;wishes,
 you do not act unless you have satisfied yourself that they do. However, in circumstances where you have legal authority to act notwithstanding that it is not possible to obtain or ascertain the instructions of your&nbsp;client,
 then you are subject to the overriding obligation to protect your&nbsp;client's&nbsp;best interests.”


The nature of your working relationship with the Claimant Committee and how, and how often, instructions are sought in practice.

Because of the importance of the second factor, we think you will need to consult with the case leads (perhaps in tomorrow’s
 call before taking a final view).
We think these are the type of questions you should ask.


Did PG get instructions from the Claimant Committee to sign up for the role of Lead Solicitor in the first place? &nbsp;
Was the Claimant Committee consulted in this regard?

Does the Claimant Committee consider the Lead Solicitor role as important to it and the case?

Do you have any reason to suspect that the Claimant Committee would not want you to step down as Lead Solicitors?

In general, how often does PG seek instructions from the Claimant Committee?

On what sorts of issues does PG seek instructions from the Claimant Committee?

On what sorts of issues does PG act without seeking instructions from the Claimant Committee?

It may be that the conclusion of this is that you do need to get instructions – and it also may be different for each
 claimant committee.
If you do so conclude, then the request for instructions must be put in the strongest possible terms, on the lines of:
 We very strongly recommend that we step down in the best interests of the claimants. If we do not step down, it may be difficult to continue acting for you altogether. Stepping down as Lead Sols is therefore the only feasible way we see to continue representing
 you.
Thanks
Matt

&nbsp;
