a@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Sarah Bingham &lt;sbingham@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Subject: RE: Enquiry for Additional ATE Cover – Pan-NOx Emissions Litigation&nbsp;CAUTION: This email originated from outside of the organization. Do not click links or open attachments unless you recognize the sender and know the content is safe.&nbsp;Many thanks Tom. Look forward to receipt of the other items when available.&nbsp;Kind regards,&nbsp;James Burke&nbsp;Associate Director (Solicitor)Litigation and Contingent Risk FINEX Global &nbsp;WTWOn behalf of Willis Limited, 51 Lime Street, London, EC3M 7DQ (registered number 181116) On behalf of Willis Towers Watson SA/NV, Quai des Vennes, 4020, Liège, Belgium (registered as a branch in the UK at 51 Lime Street, London, EC3M 7DQ UK Branch Number BR021056) in relation to all EEA-regulated business&nbsp;D: +44 (0)20 31248037M: +44 (0)7831 162591&nbsp;james.burke2@wtwco.com www.wtwco.com&nbsp;Financial, Professional, and Executive Risks (FINEX)Your Trusted Partner in Managing Complex Risk: Expertly navigating Litigation Risk, Cyber, Directors &amp; Officers, Financial Institutions, Mergers &amp; Acquisitions, Professional Indemnity, and Operational Risks within WTW. Part of our wider risk solutions capabilities at WTW&nbsp;At WTW we work flexibly - whilst you may receive emails from me out of working hours, I do not expect a response or action outside your own working hours.&nbsp;&nbsp;From: Tom Crow &lt;tcrow@pogustgoodhead.com&gt; Sent: 27 October 2025 14:04To: James Burke &lt;James.Burke2@wtwco.com&gt;Cc: Jonathan Simon &lt;Jonathan.Simon@wtwco.com&gt;; Helena Yearwood &lt;hyearwood@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Anna Varga &lt;avarga@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Sarah Bingham &lt;sbingham@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Subject: RE: Enquiry for Additional ATE Cover – Pan-NOx Emissions Litigation&nbsp;Hi James &nbsp;Let us know when works for a call, we will aim to accommodate. &nbsp;Responses are being reviewed for 2 and 3, in the meantime please see below for 1: &nbsp;1. The original LOI approach on 30 September 2025 as was:&nbsp;1.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; 50% of the Defendants’ claimed incurred costs (including Non T3 Quantum costs);2.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; 50% of the Defendants’ claimed cost of cost budgeting;&nbsp; 3.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; 100% of all approved costs to 31 July 2026 (excluding the Tranche 3 PTR, Trial Preparation and Trial phases future approved costs); and 4.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; 0% of all non-approved pending phase costs (Tranche 3 Expert Reports phase and Second General ADR Settlement phase).&nbsp;&nbsp;The updated LOI approach now adopted (based on Cost Counsel advice to be cautious) is:&nbsp;1.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; 60% of the Defendants’ claimed incurred costs (including Non T3 Quantum costs);2.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; 60% of the Defendants’ claimed cost of cost budgeting;&nbsp; 3.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; 100% of all approved costs to 31 July 2026 (including Tranche 3 PTR phase in full, 50% of Trial Preparation&nbsp; phase but still excluding Trial phase future approved costs); and4.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; 100% of all offered non-approved pending costs for Tranche 3 Expert Reports phase and Second General ADR Settlement phase.&nbsp;We decided to calculate the adverse costs exposure to 31 July 2026 rather than to the conclusion of the budgeted period (December 2026). This is informed by the indication given by Cockerill J on Day 2 of the July PDD PTR that the parties should expect the PDD Judgment to be handed down by July 2026. &nbsp;These percentages of how much work would be incurred by July 2026 are applied not only to the pre-existing Tranche 2 (PDD Trial) and First General cost budgets but also to the Tranche 3 (Quantum Trial) and Second General cost budgets. The calculations exclude the Defendants' costs contained within the Tranche 1 KBA cost budget, as they were unsuccessful in that trial. &nbsp;A further assumption applied is that, in the absence of Court-approved budgeted amounts for certain phases, specifically, Tranche 3 Expert Reports and Second General ADR/Settlement Discussions, the Claimants’ offered figures have been adopted on the basis that these constitute a reasonable estimate of the amounts likely to be approved. Approval of these phases to take place after the January 2026 CMC. &nbsp;Proceeding on the assumption that Judgment in the PDD Trial will be handed down by July 2026 and given that the Tranche 2 PDD and Tranche 3 Quantum cost budgets run concurrently up to that date, it is expected that work relating to the forthcoming Quantum Trial will need to be undertaken irrespective of the outcome.&nbsp;By 31 July 2026, all work undertaken pursuant to Tranche 2 and the First General Costs Budget will have been fully incurred, as the relevant budgeted period runs from June 2024 to April 2026, concluding with the completion of the Spring PDD Trial. In respect of the Tranche 3 and the Second General Costs Budgets it has been necessary to assess the adverse costs exposure by reference to the anticipated progress within each phase as at that date.&nbsp;By 31 July 2026, work would have been fully concluded by the Parties in all of the following phases:&nbsp;·&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Tranche 3 CMC·&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Tranche 3 Selection of the Sample Claimants·&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Tranche 3 Individual Statements of Case·&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Tranche 3 Disclosure·&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Tranche 3 Witness Statements·&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Tranche 3 Expert Reports·&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Tranche 3 PTR&nbsp;At this point, Tranche 3 Co-Ordination Costs will remain outstanding for the working phases. Applying a pro-rata apportionment, 12 months of the 17-month budgeted period will have elapsed, representing approximately 71% of the budget for that phase incurred pre-31 July 2026. &nbsp;The only other phases that will not, at least in part, have concluded by 31 July 2026 are the Tranche 3 Quantum Trial Preparation and Tranche 3 Quantum Trial phases. However, it is foreseeable that the parties will begin to incur costs in respect of counsel’s brief fees for the Quantum Trial from at least Spring 2026 onwards. On that basis, we have adopted the conservative assumption that approximately 50% of this Tranche 3 Quantum Trial Preparation phase will also have been incurred at this point. &nbsp;Similarly, in relation to the Second General Costs Budget, which spans the period April 2026 to December 2026, a comparable pro-rata analysis indicates that, as of 31 July 2026, approximately 44% of the approved costs will have been incurred in the following&nbsp; phases:&nbsp;·&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Second General GLO Management Costs (or Pan-NOx Litigation Management Costs)·&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Second General ADR/Settlement Discussions (not approved yet but LOI based on what the Claimants have offered)&nbsp;For Second General Fortnightly Meetings, we anticipate that these will run from the commencement of the budgeted period (April 2026) but will conclude in September 2026 and give way to the Quantum Trial. Accordingly, by 31 July 2026, approximately 4 of the 6 months of that phase will have elapsed, representing 67% of the approved amounts incurred.&nbsp;Applying these percentages to the budgets across the GLOs, and in accordance with the percentage cohort share that PG has aggregated on the respective Group Register, produces the total projected adverse costs exposure. Deducting the current LOI value from this figure yields the required top-up amount and consequently, the premiums we seek to incept on a staged basis to align with the progressive materialisation of adverse costs exposure throughout the litigation timetable. &nbsp;While we maintain that it is neither necessary nor proportionate at this stage to incept cover extending to the conclusion of the entire budgeted period (December 2026), we note that, in the event the Claimants are successful at the PDD Trial, approximately £83.7 million in PG adverse costs cover will become available as security. Nevertheless, we acknowledge that it would be imprudent to rely solely upon that anticipated outcome, nor would such reliance likely meet with the Court’s or the Defendants’ approval. Hence we accept that it is appropriate to implement further ATE top-ups on a staged and proportionate basis in advance of this point.&nbsp;In the event that the Claimants are unsuccessful following the handing down of the Judgment, the question arises as to whether the full extent of the insurance would be required to satisfy any adverse costs order, or whether such an order is likely to fall below the total coverage available. Although the top-up calculations have been prepared on the basis of the precise amount that we consider necessary to provide sufficient cover based on the Court-approved figures, the application of the 60% / 60% / 100% represents a measured balance between caution and pragmatism. Accordingly, it is considered that, in the event of an adverse outcome, there are reasonable grounds to expect that any adverse costs order or judgment may fall below the total level of cover currently projected.&nbsp;The first consideration is that the present calculations reflect the maximum potential adverse costs exposure, based on the approved budgeted figures. Such exposure would only arise in the event that the Defendants were wholly successful at the PDD trial on all pleaded issues. Should the Defendants succeed only in part, this would reduce the total sum payable pursuant to any adverse costs order.&nbsp;Furthermore, the calculations proceed on the assumption that the Defendants would be entitled to recover their approved budgeted costs, to the extent that such costs have in fact been incurred. However, it would not be reasonable to assume that they would recover all their non-budgeted costs at a rate of 60% upon detailed assessment. As a result, it can be reasonably be expected that a portion of the LOI may remain unused.&nbsp;Let us know if you have any queries &nbsp;​​​​Tom&nbsp;CrowGroup&nbsp;Financial&nbsp;Controller+44&nbsp;(0)&nbsp;7890&nbsp;043&nbsp;708This email and any attachments are confidential and may also be privileged. If you receive this message in error, please contact the sender immediately, destroy the email and any attachments and do not use, copy, store or disclose this email and any attachments for any purpose. Pogust Goodhead does not accept service of documents by electronic means without express prior agreement.&nbsp;Pogust Goodhead is the trading name of&nbsp;PGMBM Law Ltd, a&nbsp;limited liability company registered in England and Wales (no. 6284764) with its&nbsp;registered office at&nbsp;53-64 Chancery House, Chancery Lane, London WC2A 1QS&nbsp;and authorised and regulated by the Solicitors' Regulation Authority. We use the term ‘partner’ to refer to an employee or consultant of Pogust Goodhead with equivalent standing or qualifications. No&nbsp;reference to a partner should be taken to imply partnership for the purposes of the Partnership Act 1890&nbsp;or Limited Liability Partnerships Act 2000. A list of partners is available on our&nbsp;website.&nbsp;For details about what personal information we collect and why, please see our&nbsp;Privacy Policy.From: James Burke &lt;James.Burke2@wtwco.com&gt; Sent: 24 October 2025 16:56To: Tom Crow &lt;tcrow@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Cc: Jonathan Simon &lt;Jonathan.Simon@wtwco.com&gt;; Helena Yearwood &lt;hyearwood@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Anna Varga &lt;avarga@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Sarah Bingham &lt;sbingham@pogustgoodh