, Chancery Lane, London WC2A 1QS&nbsp;and authorised and regulated by the Solicitors' Regulation Authority. We use the term ‘partner’ to refer to an employee or consultant of Pogust Goodhead with equivalent standing or qualifications. No&nbsp;reference to a partner should be taken to imply partnership for the purposes of the Partnership Act 1890&nbsp;or Limited Liability Partnerships Act 2000. A list of partners is available on our&nbsp;website.&nbsp;For details about what personal information we collect and why, please see our&nbsp;Privacy Policy.From: James Burke &lt;James.Burke2@wtwco.com&gt; Sent: 05 November 2025 13:33To: Helena Yearwood &lt;hyearwood@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Jonathan Simon &lt;Jonathan.Simon@wtwco.com&gt;; Anna Varga &lt;avarga@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Matt Hunt &lt;mhunt@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Matthew Newbould &lt;mnewbould@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Cc: Tom Crow &lt;tcrow@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Carolina Koschdoski de Souza &lt;csouza@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Dilan Alma &lt;dalma@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Subject: RE: Enquiry for Additional ATE Cover – Pan-NOx Emissions Litigation - Toremis&nbsp;CAUTION: This email originated from outside of the organization. Do not click links or open attachments unless you recognize the sender and know the content is safe.&nbsp;Thanks for your email Helena.&nbsp;I have spoken to Toremis this morning to flesh out how they foresee the cross-collateralisation point working and confirm a few of your queries.They are happy for client proceeds to be ring-fenced. Very much like the other cross-collateralisation documents you have in place they foresee the cross-collateralisation attaching to PG’s proceeds of the claims (i.e. those monies PG receives once the proceeds have moved through the waterfall of each PD). Whilst there would be an individual excess policy attaching to each matter, the cross-collateralisation document would work so it is as if there is one overarching policy sitting over all of these top-ups, so Toremis could apply any recoveries against a loss on one particular policy. For example, if there was a £2m claim on the Ford top-up but all the other matters recovered proceeds, these proceeds would be applied to that £2m loss to make Toremis whole. We see this structure being advantageous to PG as: i.) overall the cost of insurance will be reduced and ii.) all matters will be covered – Toremis would not cherry-pick the strongest matters and only offer insurance against these. Separately Toremis would need to be added to the individual PDs in this matter which is the usual process when an excess layer is added. They would slot into the equivalent position of a 2nd/3rd/4th layer insurer (as applicable).Toremis are aware of the pre-existing cross-collateralisation provisions (Nathan Hull was head of ATE at VALE when these documents were put in place). They are comfortable with the fact that they would sit below these (as to do otherwise would need to unwind the provisions that are in place).@Anna Varga @Matt Hunt,@Matthew Newbould Re the merits call later – Toremis have no particular queries the case leads would need to be aware of. It will essentially be fleshing out the bullet-point queries sent to Counsel (below) / giving a “state of the nation” walk-through of the merits of the matter, similar to the call conducted with the existing insurers ahead of the PDD trial.&nbsp;Confirming, at a high level, that, with the benefit of having now reviewed the expert and factual evidence (and the detailed legal position taken by the defendants) the merits remain at least as good as the original assessments (including confirming what the relevant percentage assessment is for each of the defendants).Confirming the position on the merits in relation to: (1) whether each of the defendants' vehicles contained unlawful defeat devices; (2) whether there are any viable article 5 defences available to the defendants; and (3) to the extent separate to (1) and (2), the position on the existen