Subject:&nbsp;Wish List &amp; Requests

&nbsp;


Hi Anna,

&nbsp;

Following on from yesterday and the calls with LD, a few things we would suggest are raised with&nbsp;Martyn. Firstly, it is important to say the Case Leads and PG team are wildly
 committed to this trial. That should not be in question. However, the AA (the executed version of which the case leads were only sent at 9.22AM this morning), which has been referred to in Court documents and has been incepted without the consent of those
 persons it applies to (either expressly in the case of the Case Leads or, as far as the wider team is concerned, by implication), causes concern. Additionally, the case leads have also raised serious concerns about the staff's &nbsp;mental health that are yet to
 be addressed by the firm.

&nbsp;

The AA was incepted with little to no visibility or consideration for how it will practically work (as is clear given Martyn's explanation in ​the call on 8 October was substantially
 different to our understanding of what it means, a view which we consider you, also on that call, shared). We need further clarity on the AA. This is true for both for the Case Leads, and the PG staff who will be indirectly subject to it - by way of taking
 instructions from the Case Leads who are under the supervision of Leigh Day. &nbsp;As we have already told you, there is a real risk of the substantive PG team being entirely alienated by this agreement. These concerns were evidently ignored (including our concerns
 over the mental health and well-being of the staff) given it was executed without further recourse to us. &nbsp;It also remains unclear what, if any, interaction LD is to have with the wider PG team, if they are not subject to the AA (and therefore direction and
 supervision), and whether, given this, the PG team would need to revert to one of the Case Leads for every interaction, instruction, discussion had with the LD team. An obvious example of this is the trial bundle/logistics workstreams which have largely been
 left between the excellent PG associates and LD with limited input from the case leads. It is fair to say the PG team carries that workstream and LD are unprepared to take it on. Similarly with Opus and issues at this morning's hearing.&nbsp;

&nbsp;

It would be helpful for an explanation, and for the physical AA, to be sent out to the wider PG team&nbsp;along with some practical&nbsp;guidance about what it means. It would be inappropriate
 for the Case Leads to do this given: i) we haven’t agreed to the AA; and ii) it has been incepted against our consent.

&nbsp;

It would be helpful if the explanation covered:

&nbsp;


what LD expect from the PG team&nbsp; - do they simply expect the PG teams to &quot;do what they're told, when they're told&quot;, or is there still going to be a collaborative approach
 (the former seems more likely given how things have happened over the last few days);&nbsp;
how the &quot;do what they're told, when they're told&quot; works - or, indeed wider interaction works, in circumstances where the team isn't caught by the agreement.
 Will they need to take each instruction and run it past a Case Lead?
what happens in circumstances where the PG teams disagree with LDs position on decisions? How does this get resolved between the firms? We stress this is not a hypothetical.
 Aside from wider case strategy we have had immediate differences in opinion last week in terms of costs, with decisions being made and taken without reference to PG. This impacts all claimants, and, of course PG's clients. Further, in terms of wider points,
 as we have pointed out, LD do not have clients on some of the secondary or ALGLO cases, all of which are bound by the PDD trial judgment. It is easy to anticipate a diversion of opinion/strategy as the PDD trial develops which could impact PG only cases/clients
 and for which, under the AA the Case Leads cannot wear two hats.&nbsp;&nbsp;
how working practices are going to change, both outwardly to the Ds and Court etc, and whether a working group should be set up to standardise approach e.g. do all
 things on the PG SharePoint move across to LD - what is the timeframe, how is it co-ordinated? Whilst an administrative concern this does have significance in terms of sheer volume and also maintaining/transferring admin rights for links particularly where
 the Defendants and the Court already retain access.&nbsp;
How does it work for the costs team?&nbsp;

&nbsp;

Each of these, along with sight of the AA, will go some way to helping the PG staff understand the agreement they are now working under and hopefully to stop the wheels from
 falling off. This will also assist in answering questions the Case Leads have, but it will not, we doubt, satisfy any regulatory concerns we have, and we request again the Board's complete answer to our note of 05.10.2025 (under cover of email of Sarah Bingham).
 To date we have not had any answer from you.&nbsp;

&nbsp;

On this point, we thought it helpful to also compile a few outstanding requests from our end to you / the Board. We should also say thank you again for speaking to Alicia
 and arranging the call with the employment solicitor. While we hope this will assist in clarifying the position in terms of whether this AA can be imposed upon us and not contravene our employment contracts, it will not be able to answer any of our regulatory
 concerns (in respect of the AA and wider regulatory concerns). These really can only be answered by the following:&nbsp;

&nbsp;


the advice from BWKC or any other counsel on the AA. This is especially true where many of the concerns we have raised overlap with those in the HSFK letter - including
 the regulatory position for us as lawyers, and the position on client consent. We still have not consented to the AA and we all have significant regulatory concerns. In particular we have not seen any advice which contradicts our position that client consent
 is necessary for the AA.
Re the AA and how strategic decisions are made, a very practical question: in circumstances where LD and PG (either collaboratively or by PG being informed) make a
 decision, do the PG bods have to in turn report these to the Board / AV? Do we have to report where decisions have been made that we do not agree with? What is our internal reporting matrix for issues such as this?&nbsp;
Will our COLP, in their role as COLP, be giving us sign off on the agreement, in addition to the Counsel advice?
As our COLP is now also a case lead (for Pan NOx and it appears individual cases), is there a delineation of the COLP roles, from the role of case lead, and CEO of
 the firm? How is this safeguarded?

&nbsp;

If these questions are not answered to a satisfactory standard, then our regulatory concerns will remain and as regulated persons, we will need to consider our positions
 and what, if any, further steps we need to take.

&nbsp;

Thanks,

&nbsp;

Case Leads

