ior agreement.&nbsp;Pogust Goodhead is the trading name of&nbsp;PGMBM Law Ltd, a&nbsp;limited liability company registered in England and Wales (no. 6284764) with its&nbsp;registered office at&nbsp;53-64 Chancery House, Chancery Lane, London WC2A 1QS&nbsp;and authorised and regulated by the Solicitors' Regulation Authority. We use the term ‘partner’ to refer to an employee or consultant of Pogust Goodhead with equivalent standing or qualifications. No&nbsp;reference to a partner should be taken to imply partnership for the purposes of the Partnership Act 1890&nbsp;or Limited Liability Partnerships Act 2000. A list of partners is available on our&nbsp;website.&nbsp;For details about what personal information we collect and why, please see our&nbsp;Privacy Policy.From: Carolina Koschdoski de Souza &lt;csouza@pogustgoodhead.com&gt; Sent: 12 November 2025 13:08To: Tom Crow &lt;tcrow@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Nick Paolazzi &lt;npaolazzi@gramercy.com&gt;; Joshua Katz &lt;jkatz@gramercy.com&gt;Cc: James Taylor &lt;JTaylor@gramercy.com&gt;; Marc Zelina &lt;mzelina@gramercy.com&gt;; Josh O'Melia &lt;jomelia@gramercy.com&gt;; Alicia Alinia &lt;aalinia@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Donatas Juskauskas &lt;djuskauskas@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Helena Yearwood &lt;hyearwood@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Joseph Moreno &lt;jmoreno@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Howard Morris &lt;hmorris@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Subject: RE: Enquiry for Additional ATE Cover – Pan-NOx Emissions Litigation - ToremisImportance: High&nbsp;Dear Gramercy,&nbsp;Please find below a quick update on the discussions with Toremis. &nbsp;They confirmed they have all they need on the merits to finalise their analysis. &nbsp;On the cross-collateralisation issue, they are seeking similar obligations from PG to hold back the proceeds until the final resolution of the cases. See their response below.&nbsp;“In short, our view is that for our cross-collateralisation arrangements, where there is settlement or payment, there will need to be a holdback of funds by PG, sufficient to cover the outstanding limit on Toremis policies at the time that the settlement funds are received by PG. Those funds should then be held until the Toremis policies come off risk (which, given the stage of the proceedings, should occur within a short period of time).&nbsp;Can PG confirm that this is agreed in principle, subject to addressing the wording to achieve this?”&nbsp;We expect them to seek wording that is similar to the mechanism in clause 1(b) of the BHP 4th excess reimbursement deed. It will be difficult to justify a circumstance where it has been given in relation to the BHP 4th excess reimbursement deed but not for Toremis’ proposal, where the intention behind both structures is the same.&nbsp;Could you please confirm that this works? In the meantime, we asked if they could agree in principle to cap the amount to be provisioned/ring fenced by PG, as we have both in the wrapper deed and in clause 1(b) of the 4th layer reimbursement deed. &nbsp;We appreciate your prompt attention to this matter, given the time constraints.&nbsp;Kind regards &nbsp;​​​​Carolina&nbsp;Koschdoski&nbsp;de&nbsp;SouzaLegal&nbsp;DirectorFrom: Tom Crow &lt;tcrow@pogustgoodhead.com&gt; Sent: 11 November 2025 15:22To: Nick Paolazzi &lt;npaolazzi@gramercy.com&gt;; Carolina Koschdoski de Souza &lt;csouza@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Joshua Katz &lt;jkatz@gramercy.com&gt;Cc: James Taylor &lt;JTaylor@gramercy.com&gt;; Marc Zelina &lt;mzelina@gramercy.com&gt;; Josh O'Melia &lt;jomelia@gramercy.com&gt;; Alicia Alinia &lt;aalinia@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Donatas Juskauskas &lt;djuskauskas@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Helena Yearwood &lt;hyearwood@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Subject: RE: Enquiry for Additional ATE Cover – Pan-NOx Emissions Litigation - Toremis&nbsp;Thanks Nick &nbsp;Just sent the invite&nbsp;Tom&nbsp;​​​​Tom&nbsp;CrowGroup&nbsp;Financial&nbsp;Controller+44&nbsp;(0)&nbsp;7890&nbsp;043&nbsp;708From: Nick Paolazzi &lt;npaolazzi@gramercy.com&gt; Sent: 11 November 2025 18:20To: Tom Crow &lt;tcrow@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Carolina Koschdoski de Souza &lt;csouza@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Joshua Katz &lt;jkatz@gramercy.com&gt;Cc: James Taylor &lt;JTaylor@gramercy.com&gt;; Marc Zelina &lt;mzelina@gramercy.com&gt;; Josh O'Melia &lt;jomelia@gramercy.com&gt;; Alicia Alinia &lt;aalinia@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Donatas Juskauskas &lt;djuskauskas@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Helena Yearwood &lt;hyearwood@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Subject: Re: Enquiry for Additional ATE Cover – Pan-NOx Emissions Litigation - Toremis&nbsp;CAUTION: This email originated from outside of the organization. Do not click links or open attachments unless you recognize the sender and know the content is safe.&nbsp;Let’s do 1:45 if that works for you&nbsp;From: Tom Crow &lt;tcrow@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Sent: Tuesday, November 11, 2025 1:08:55 PMTo: Carolina Koschdoski de Souza &lt;csouza@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Nick Paolazzi &lt;npaolazzi@gramercy.com&gt;; Joshua Katz &lt;jkatz@gramercy.com&gt;Cc: James Taylor &lt;JTaylor@gramercy.com&gt;; Marc Zelina &lt;mzelina@gramercy.com&gt;; Josh O'Melia &lt;jomelia@gramercy.com&gt;; Alicia Alinia &lt;aalinia@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Donatas Juskauskas &lt;djuskauskas@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Helena Yearwood &lt;hyearwood@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Subject: RE: Enquiry for Additional ATE Cover – Pan-NOx Emissions Litigation - Toremis &nbsp;[External Email] Hi Nick, Josh&nbsp;Do you have availability today to run through the Nox ATE waterfall model?&nbsp;We are under pressure to secure a binding quote by the end of the week so that we can make a statement to this effect in our SfC hearing skeleton argument so need to keep the wheels moving&nbsp;I am free at your availability, draft model attached&nbsp;Tom&nbsp;​​​​Tom&nbsp;CrowGroup&nbsp;Financial&nbsp;Controller+44&nbsp;(0)&nbsp;7890&nbsp;043&nbsp;708This email and any attachments are confidential and may also be privileged. If you receive this message in error, please contact the sender immediately, destroy the email and any attachments and do not use, copy, store or disclose this email and any attachments for any purpose. Pogust Goodhead does not accept service of documents by electronic means without express prior agreement.&nbsp;Pogust Goodhead is the trading name of&nbsp;PGMBM Law Ltd, a&nbsp;limited liability company registered in England and Wales (no. 6284764) with its&nbsp;registered office at&nbsp;53-64 Chancery House, Chancery Lane, London WC2A 1QS&nbsp;and authorised and regulated by the Solicitors' Regulation Authority. We use the term ‘partner’ to refer to an employee or consultant of Pogust Goodhead with equivalent standing or qualifications. No&nbsp;reference to a partner should be taken to imply partnership for the purposes of the Partnership Act 1890&nbsp;or Limited Liability Partnerships Act 2000. A list of partners is available on our&nbsp;website.&nbsp;For details about what personal information we collect and why, please see our&nbsp;Privacy Policy.From: Carolina Koschdoski de Souza &lt;csouza@pogustgoodhead.com&gt; Sent: 07 November 2025 22:30To: Nick Paolazzi &lt;npaolazzi@gramercy.com&gt;; Joshua Katz &lt;jkatz@gramercy.com&gt;; Tom Crow &lt;tcrow@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Cc: James Taylor &lt;jtaylor@gramercy.com&gt;; Marc Zelina &lt;mzelina@gramercy.com&gt;; Josh O'Melia &lt;jomelia@gramercy.com&gt;; Alicia Alinia &lt;aalinia@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Donatas Juskauskas &lt;djuskauskas@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Helena Yearwood &lt;hyearwood@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Subject: RE: Enquiry for Additional ATE Cover – Pan-NOx Emissions Litigation - Toremis&nbsp;Nick and Josh,&nbsp;Following our call today, we would like to update you further on PG’s discussion with Toremis about the ATE top-ups for emissions and initiate the approval process required for compliance with the Credit Agreements of 2 October 2023, 23 August 2024, and 14 August 2025 (“Credit Agreements”).&nbsp;Structure Overview&nbsp;Gramercy Guarantee&nbsp;We confirmed that obtaining a guarantee from Gramercy would be challenging, and Toremis appeared comfortable proceeding without it, provided that the structure outlined below is acceptable.&nbsp;Cross-CollateralisationThis is a potential dealbreaker for at least some of the cases requiring top-ups. Toremis is seeking a mechanism akin to the existing overarching deed (“Wrapper”) but limited to cross-collateralisation among the Nox cases only.&nbsp;Because the risk profile varies across the Nox cases, they have proposed a separate cross-collateralisation agreement that would apply to the eight top-up policies. Toremis is aware of the existing cross-collateralisation provisions within the Wrapper and is comfortable that the new arrangement would rank below these, as altering the current priority would require changes to the existing structure.&nbsp;We will need to discuss further how the cross-collateralisation of the additional layers would coexist within the waterfall, particularly in relation to the cross-collateralisation of emissions proceeds in the 4th layer of the BHP ATE policy (as formalised in the Reimbursement Deed), which sets out payment of reimbursement out of 50% of PG net proceeds from Nox cases after taxes, insurers, counsel, and payments to funders, including Gramercy facilities.&nbsp;Economic Terms of the Top-Up&nbsp;The upfront premium, D&amp;C, reimbursement of adverse costs, and other terms and conditions would mirror those of the existing layers of the ATE policies and the individual Priorities Deeds. This additional layer would sit above the current layers with respect to reimbursements and D&amp;C premiums payable within each Nox case.&nbsp;As discussed, to cover insurers’ risk arising from the AAE to be given to defendants, they would require PG to provide an indemnity for any potential claims paid by the insurers that fall within the exclusion circumstances set out in the ATE policies — effectively operating as a clawback mechanism. This indemnity would also require approval from the Gramercy lenders under the Credit Agreements.&nbsp;Next Steps&nbsp;The next step is for PG to share a simplified model illustrating how the case proceeds would flow and how the cross-collateralisation would operate. It was unclear from the call whether Toremis is fully across the details of the mechanics of the overarching Priorities Deed currently in place. Toremis expects to provide us with a quote by the end of next week, and we will need to be prepared to move quickly to incept the policies.&nbsp;Approvals&nbsp;In light of the above, we would like to start the process for approval by the Gramercy lenders to ensure compliance with Section 6.21 regarding the amounts defined as “Third Party Fees” (described in Schedule III). We understand that Gramercy may also need to seek consent from the CPI insurers. Could you please confirm how long that process is expected to take? Please let us know if there is anything else we can do to facilitate or expedite the necessary approvals at this stage.&nbsp;Kind regards,&nbsp;PG&nbsp;​​​​Carolina&nbsp;Koschdoski&nbsp;de&nbsp;SouzaLegal&nbsp;DirectorFrom: Tom Crow &lt;tcrow@pogustgoodhead.com&gt; Sent: 06 November 2025 15:43To: Nick Paolazzi &lt;npaolazzi@gramercy.com&gt;Cc: Donatas Juskauskas &lt;djuskauskas@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Carolina Koschdoski de Souza &lt;csouza@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Helena Yearwood &lt;hyearwood@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Subject: FW: Enquiry for Additional ATE Cover – Pan-NOx Emissions Litigation - Toremis&nbsp;Hi Nick &nbsp;We have received some further queries from our brokers regarding the proposed topup to the Nox policies (+£17m LOI)&nbsp;They want a call tomorrow to discuss 1b, would it be possible to discuss with you on a quick call before hand?&nbsp;Query from insurer below, let me know if you are available &nbsp;TomOn the economics - we will review the existing cross-collateralisation arrangements with a view to how they might be adapted for our purposes. We do have two requests on this side of things though:We note that for the existing arrangements, PG is the only claimant-side party. Our current view is that we would also want Gramercy as a party, either in its own right or as a guarantor. Is this acceptable to PG/Gramercy?On a related point, we would like to better understand how any settlement proceeds would flow through the existing waterfall(s), including the arrangements with cash flow up to Gramercy. Essentially, we would like to be able to understand what the effect of, for example, a settlement with Mercedes, would have on the money flowing through the waterfall in order to assess the extent to which such a settlement would then protect us from adverse results against other defendants. It would also be useful to be able to see the effect of varying levels of settlement. We assume that PG has excel models which would allow us to understand this - could we see copies?&nbsp;&nbsp;​​​​Tom&nbsp;CrowGroup&nbsp;Financial&nbsp;Controller+44&nbsp;(0)&nbsp;7890&nbsp;043&nbsp;708From: James Burke &lt;James.Burke2@wtwco.com&gt; Sent: 06 November 2025 11:09To: Tom Crow &lt;tcrow@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Helena Yearwood &lt;hyearwood@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Jonathan Simon &lt;Jonathan.Simon@wtwco.com&gt;; Anna Varga &lt;avarga@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Matthew Newbould &lt;mnewbould@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Cc: Carolina Koschdoski de Souza &lt;csouza@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Dilan Alma &lt;dalma@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Subject: RE: Enquiry for Additional ATE Cover – Pan-NOx Emissions Litigation - Toremis&nbsp;CAUTION: This email originated from outside of the organization. Do not click links or open attachments unless you recognize the sender and know the content is safe.&nbsp;Thanks Tom.&nbsp;I’ve been in touch with Toremis by email this morning and they have sent the following update. Would you be able to assist with their request please:I think we have all we need from you/PG on the merits now. We just need to reflect internally on the overall 