Hi all,


&nbsp;


Just in response to the email below re: cost&nbsp;points:


&nbsp;


a) Cost of the application for PG stepping down temporarily as Lead Sols on Lead Cases.
Unless I am mistaken, a formal application to amend the GLO should not be required to remove Pogust Goodhead as Lead Solicitors. If all parties agree, this can be dealt with by way of consent order and submitted for court approval prior to the PTR. In that
 scenario, any adverse costs for amending the GLO would be minimal, if any.


If an application has to be made because the Defendants will not agree by consent, then the normal costs consequences apply i.e. costs follow the event. If the Defendants unreasonably
 refuse, we should not be liable for their adverse costs. If, however, they say they will only agree by consent if PG pays their costs incurred since Thursday’s announcement, those costs ought to be minimal. As with any offer for adverse costs, we would need
 to take client instructions, but the costs exposure can be mitigated if the application is made sooner rather than later.


b) Ability to recover common costs as Lead Sols.


Common costs are recoverable inter partes by reference to the approved costs budgets. The default position under each GLO is that Lead Solicitors conduct the common costs work. However,
 the cost-sharing provisions allow such work to be delegated (with prior authorisation) to a Non-Lead Solicitor. This is what has already occurred in Ford, Renault-Nissan, and Citroën-Peugeot with Milberg London LLP.

For example, the Mercedes GLO [43] states:
With the exception of such preparation for and attendance at meetings of the Claimants' Solicitors Group as is reasonably required, no further work in relation to the Common Issues shall be undertaken by any legal representative other than the members of the
 Steering Committee, their servants or agents unless authorised in advance by the Steering Committee;&nbsp;and no liability for Common Costs in relation to such work in the absence of such authorisation shall arise between
 the Claimants or between the parties.

This may&nbsp;therefore require an update to clients to explain that the default position has changed; however, we can also explain the mechanism under the GLO,&nbsp;which permits Non-Lead firms to conduct common work when authorised.


c) CFA deductions.


I do not think the judgment in 
Julia Mazur v Charles Russell Speechlys LLP&nbsp;[2025] EWHC 2341 (KB) should cause concern. Paralegal work is recoverable in costs provided that:

(a) it does not involve conducting “reserved legal activities” independently;
(b) it is supervised by an authorised individual; and
(c) it is reasonable and proportionate in nature and amount.


None of our paralegals conduct reserved legal activities within the meaning of section 12(1) of the Legal Services Act 2007. These activities are limited to:




the exercise of a right of audience;




the conduct of litigation (issuing a claim form, signing statements of truth, filing a defence/response, etc.);




reserved instrument activities (e.g. drafting a transfer of land, completing registration documents for the Land Registry);




probate activities;




notarial activities; or




administration of oaths.


Accordingly, PG stepping down as Lead Solicitors does not prevent the firm from being instructed under the GLO to conduct common work and does not impact the level of CFA deductions that may be applied to client
 damages.

Best,


Sarah


