of Juston Smithers (the Mercedes Claimant's automotive engineering expert). Substantive, and supplementary, reports were prepared and filed by each of these experts over July and August 2025.&nbsp;&nbsp;The applications made by the Defendants were largely on similar grounds: they argued that Dr Heitz stepped outside the remit of his instruction and provided expert evidence that should be left to the mechanical engineer i.e. he provided duplicative evidence; that the Claimants did not have permission to rely on the evidence of Dr Tate in any litigation other than Mercedes; and that the Claimants did not have permission to rely on the evidence of Mr Smithers in any litigation other than Mercedes. These applications were heard on a one day hearing on 10 September 2025 and while we did seek to reach agreement where we could, largely they were opposed.&nbsp;In her judgment, Mrs Justice Cockerill was scathing in its criticism of the reports, seemingly displaying a real unhappiness over the fact this hearing was even required, but made some notable points about what the reports could continue to have in them (which were helpful for the Claimants).&nbsp;&nbsp;As a result of these &quot;lost&quot; applications, the Defendants made the point that they would be seeking their costs of the applications on an indemnity basis. While the Court did not indicate it would award costs on an indemnity basis, our suspicion is that if we do not reach an agreement with the Defendants for the total amount of costs to be paid, and the Defendants make an application for a costs order on a indemnity basis, they will be successful.&nbsp;The costs sought by the Defendants are as follows:&nbsp;&nbsp;ReportMercedesRenaultNissanFordPCDTotalHeitz£392,755.71£124,867.13£421,034.11£212,398.41£175,000£1,326,055.36Tate£70,543.30£15,478.82&nbsp;£24,9"}

