Dear Michael,

&nbsp;
Thank you for your email.&nbsp; I have also been provided with your emails to Alicia from today regarding a potential article in&nbsp;The Times, suggestions of criminal conduct and concerns
 over Gramercy.&nbsp; This email responds to them&nbsp;together.
&nbsp;
As previously communicated to you, we will not be releasing you from your notice period.&nbsp; Your continued attempts to leave earlier than your contract allows are proving disruptive
 to our efforts to safeguard good governance within the firm.&nbsp;&nbsp;In addition, your conduct threatens to seriously damage morale and be more disruptive than helpful, taking account of the best interests of our clients.
&nbsp;
We are, therefore, minded to&nbsp;give you the option of being placed&nbsp;on&nbsp;paid leave for the remainder of your notice period.&nbsp; We would view that as regrettable since we are in the midst
 of the PDD Trial and would much prefer to have you remain&nbsp;working&nbsp;on the team.&nbsp;&nbsp;
&nbsp;
We therefore give you the choice of either to (i) stay working throughout the remainder of your notice period or (ii) go on&nbsp;paid&nbsp;leave effective immediately.
&nbsp;
If you continue to try to exit early,&nbsp;given the unparticularised nature of your allegations, we can only conclude that you are grasping for reasons to engineer such an exit for your
 own reasons, which is unprofessional, threatens breaches of your employment contract, and is not in the best interests of our clients.&nbsp; We put you on notice that we will not hesitate to take legal action against you to enforce your employment contract (in
 particular your notice period and all covenants), nor will we hesitate to take action against any firm that might seek to employ you ahead of your notice period expiring or in breach of your covenants.
&nbsp;
If you choose to remain&nbsp;working&nbsp;at the firm, we must insist that you focus solely on your work and only raise any further concerns with sufficient particularity and justification so
 that we can understand them fully and investigate them if necessary.&nbsp; Unclear and unparticularised allegations as you have been raising for the past several weeks are simply improper and must stop.
&nbsp;
Regarding the specific points raised in your emails:
&nbsp;


We are looking into your concerns over Gramercy’s recent input into the Litigation Management Agreement and Agency Agreements. &nbsp;Your email was lengthy, so we need time to fully digest your concerns,
 but we intend to respond early next week.&nbsp; For the avoidance of doubt, the remainder of this email was prepared before we received your email regarding Gramercy.
Your suggestion that you cannot act in the best interests of our clients under supervision from other staff is not understood.&nbsp; If you continue to believe this is the case, please explain which members of staff and
 why, by precise reference to specific provisions of your contract or the SRA Code of Conduct, say this is so.
You refer vaguely to potential criminal conduct.&nbsp; This is obviously a very serious allegation.&nbsp; Please now state clearly and unequivocally what precise conduct you are referring to and by which specific people so
 that we can investigate immediately.
As you are aware, the firm has already taken robust action against Tom Goodhead.&nbsp; We take our obligations to our stakeholders extremely seriously and there is not, therefore, any basis for you to seek to exit early.&nbsp;
 Even if there has been improper behaviour at the Firm – and rest assured, we will investigate any such allegations seriously – that does not give you any justification, under either your contract or your regulatory obligations. Conjecture about what may or
 may not be published in&nbsp;The Times&nbsp;does not change that position.
I look forward to hearing back from you regarding whether you wish to continue working on the case&nbsp;at PG&nbsp;or go onto&nbsp;paid&nbsp;leave&nbsp;by return and in any event by 5pm on Monday 20 October
 2025. We remain hopeful that you will agree to continue to work&nbsp;at PG&nbsp;throughout the PDD Trial.
&nbsp;
In the meantime, and given your reference to protected disclosures and your suggestion that you will cease working on Monday despite the terms of your contract, please confirm that
 you have and will continue to preserve all documents that might be relevant to your employment with PG and associated covenants.
&nbsp;
All of the firm’s rights are fully reserved.
&nbsp;
