&nbsp;
Pogust Goodhead Can't Step Back From Lead Role On Dieselgate Trial
By&nbsp;Joanne Faulkner&nbsp;

Pogust Goodhead&nbsp;must continue co-leading claims
 made by millions of drivers of diesel vehicles against car manufactures after a High Court judge on Friday refused to grant the firm's application to step aside from the case heading to trial on Monday.&nbsp;

Justice Sara Cockeril said she is currently not prepared to grant an order that would allow Pogust Goodhead to remove its self as a joint lead solicitor law firm&nbsp;through an&nbsp;&quot;agency agreement&quot; with&nbsp;Leigh
 Day, the co-lead counsel who would take over as sole lead in the diesel emissions litigation.

The agency agreement would see Pogust Goodhead employees carry on their work under the direction of Leigh Day. But in her ruling,&nbsp;Justice Cockerill said the implications of approving the plan were &quot;far from clear,&quot; particularly around the question of potential
 impacts on lawyer-client relationship.

There is also a lack of clarity around whether the&nbsp;Solicitors Regulation Authority&nbsp;has
 been asked to approve the arrangement, Justice Cockerill said.

&quot;As matters stand at this point in time, the status quo appears to be the best course pending further clarification from Pogust Goodhead or approval of the proposed agency relationship by the SRA,&quot; the judge said.&nbsp;

Pogust Goodhead&nbsp;asked the court&nbsp;for permission on Oct. 6 to exit its role as a joint lead firm on one of
 largest group claims ever bought in English legal history. The application said that recent departures from the firm, including by its co-founder and the head partner on the case, risk becoming a distraction.

At least five other senior lawyers, including partner Pete Gallagher — who was overseeing the firm's team on the case — have left the firm or are working their notice.

The law firm later assured Justice Cockerill that it was&nbsp;&quot;fanciful&quot;&nbsp;to suggest that the trial could be
 put at risk because the SRA would interfere with the agreement, according to court documents seen by Law360 on Wednesday.&nbsp; The solicitors' watchdog has been engaging with Pogust Goodhead since August and has raised no issues about the managerial changes at
 the law firm and &quot;none is anticipated,&quot; the court note reads.

In a statement sent to Law360 on Sunday, a spokesperson for Pogust Goodhead welcomed Justice Cockerill's decision &quot;that the status quo is the best course at this time, which means that Pogust Goodhead will continue as lead solicitor, along with Leigh Day.&quot;&nbsp;

&quot;We are fully committed to successfully litigating this historic case and remain focused on the pursuit of justice for the millions of people affected by the U.K. diesel emissions scandal,&quot; the spokesperson said.&nbsp;

Pogust Goodhead acts for 750,000 of 1.6 million claims in the case. Leigh Day would have taken on the lead solicitor role alone under the proposed agreement. The law firm maintains it has the resourcing capacity to continue acting as lead solicitor, and will
 continue to do so and the outcome will have no impact on the trial.

The trial, which is due to last 10 weeks,&nbsp;centers on&nbsp;whether five lead defendants —&nbsp;Mercedes-Benz,
 Renault, Ford, Nissan and the&nbsp;Peugeot-Citroën group — incorporated prohibited &quot;defeat devices&quot; into
 diesel vehicles.&nbsp;

The case is Various Claimants v. Mercedes-Benz Group AG and others, case number QB-2022-002405 and others, in the King's Bench Division of the&nbsp;High
 Court of Justice&nbsp;of England and Wales.
